WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.089 --> 00:00:04.600
If you’re in the northern hemisphere, it’s
that time of year when you start to get

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:09.980
cozy, pull those sweaters out of the closet,
sip your pumpkin spice lattes, and stare out

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:13.881
the window, waiting for the leaves to start
turning orange.

00:00:13.881 --> 00:00:19.310
Well, over at microcosmos.store we have the
perfect companion for this time of year and

00:00:19.310 --> 00:00:22.736
that is our Journey To The Microcosmos sweatshirt.

00:00:22.736 --> 00:00:28.670
It’s soft, it’s cozy, and it confirms
to those around you that you are in fact human.

00:00:28.670 --> 00:00:34.110
And speaking of that pumpkin spice latte,
we sell a lovely tardigrade mug that is perfect

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:40.806
for your warm beverage of choice, or if you’ve
got 2 billion tardigrades that you need to rehome.

00:00:40.806 --> 00:00:46.711
So, check out microcosmos.store, where you
can find our sweatshirt, mug, and even our

00:00:46.711 --> 00:00:49.117
very own Microcosmos microscope.

00:01:01.635 --> 00:01:06.990
James, our master of microscopes, is always
on the hunt for rare ciliates that live in

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:09.643
areas low in oxygen.

00:01:09.643 --> 00:01:14.520
But when he gathers those samples and puts
them under a growth light, his tubes quickly

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:21.220
turn the color of the green sulfur bacteria
that thrive in those anaerobic conditions.

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:26.450
These organisms are not particularly hard
to come by, especially if you know the kinds

00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:29.477
of environments that meet their basic needs.

00:01:29.477 --> 00:01:36.090
But they can still manage to surprise us,
like in 2005, when scientists reported that

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:41.960
they had found green sulfur bacteria living
in a place that seemed impossible for them

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:43.737
to survive in:

00:01:43.737 --> 00:01:45.788
a hydrothermal vent.

00:02:00.463 --> 00:02:05.170
Now it doesn’t sound particularly comfortable
to me, but life around a hydrothermal vent

00:02:05.170 --> 00:02:07.156
is not impossible.

00:02:07.156 --> 00:02:12.000
These structures tend to form in places where
tectonic plates are shifting away from each

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:18.573
other, creating cracks that heated water from
under the crust can then erupt through.

00:02:18.573 --> 00:02:24.677
And despite the incredible heat within the
vent and the lack of light around it,

00:02:24.677 --> 00:02:26.930
there is life—

00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:35.017
an ecosystem built in part on bacteria that
are able to turn chemicals from the vent into food.

00:02:35.017 --> 00:02:41.540
These bacteria do not need sunlight to feed
themselves and the organisms that surround them.

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:47.670
But green sulfur bacteria are not like those
chemosynthetic residents that we know to expect

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:49.421
in hydrothermal vents.

00:02:49.421 --> 00:02:58.445
Green sulfur bacteria rely entirely on photosynthesis
for their food needs, which means they need light.

00:02:58.445 --> 00:03:03.959
Now to understand the secrets behind the green
sulfur bacteria’s deep sea survival, let’s

00:03:03.959 --> 00:03:08.145
start by talking about what a green sulfur
bacteria even is.

00:03:10.395 --> 00:03:16.450
These bacteria are known more formally as
the family Chlorobiaceae, and their distinguishing

00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:19.934
feature is that they abhor oxygen.

00:03:19.934 --> 00:03:25.470
To power their photosynthesis, they rely on
reduced sulfur instead, which means that green

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:30.780
sulfur bacteria prefer to live in areas of
water deep enough to favor sulfur

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:37.410
over oxygen but still allow enough light for
the bacteria to create their food.

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:42.860
Most green sulfur species cannot swim, which
is why they’re so still in our footage.

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:48.340
There are a few species that have vacuoles
that can fill or release gas to help them

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:50.890
move up and down the water column.

00:03:50.890 --> 00:03:54.151
But overall, they're not really movers.

00:03:54.151 --> 00:03:59.220
And you also may have noticed that not all of the 
bacteria in our footage is green, and that some of

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:01.737
them are in fact, purple.

00:04:01.737 --> 00:04:05.860
At times they’re quite a contrast to the
green sulfur bacteria, not just because of

00:04:05.860 --> 00:04:10.611
their color, but because they are able to
swim around.

00:04:10.611 --> 00:04:16.100
These purple neighbors do share one common
trait with green sulfur bacteria though:

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:19.470
they also use sulfur to carry out photosynthesis.

00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:23.034
And so they are known as, wait for it,

00:04:23.034 --> 00:04:25.320
purple sulfur bacteria.

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:29.557
We would call this an uncreative rip-off, 
but they were actually described 

00:04:29.557 --> 00:04:32.891
about a century before
their green counterparts.

00:04:32.891 --> 00:04:38.910
As a family, the purple sulfur bacteria are
known as Chromatiaceae, which does feel a

00:04:38.910 --> 00:04:42.080
bit more distinct as far as names go.

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:46.750
While the chemical reactions underpinning
their photosynthesis are not identical, purple

00:04:46.750 --> 00:04:53.030
and green sulfur bacteria have similar requirements
for where they live to make that photosynthesis happen.

00:04:53.030 --> 00:04:55.933
They need light and they need sulfur.

00:04:55.933 --> 00:05:00.675
So it isn’t strange to find both organisms
living on our slides together.

00:05:00.675 --> 00:05:03.669
But that doesn’t do their proximity enough
justice.

00:05:03.669 --> 00:05:08.670
These two bacteria don’t just tend to show
up in the same areas of water.

00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:14.750
Green sulfur bacteria like to live
under purple sulfur bacteria, providing sulfur

00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:16.770
to their purple upstairs neighbors.

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:24.347
In exchange, the purple sulfur bacteria shield
the green sulfur bacteria from oxygen.

00:05:24.347 --> 00:05:30.110
But of course, life isn’t necessarily easy
just because you’ve buried yourself away

00:05:30.110 --> 00:05:32.570
from anything vaguely threatening.

00:05:32.570 --> 00:05:36.999
The green sulfur bacteria still has to be
able to survive underneath its

00:05:36.999 --> 00:05:39.480
Chromatiaceae comforter.

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:45.289
It helps that green sulfur bacteria have a
larger affinity for hydrogen sulfide, ensuring

00:05:45.289 --> 00:05:49.470
they still get the chemicals they need to
power their reactions.

00:05:49.470 --> 00:05:52.970
But they still need light.

00:05:52.970 --> 00:05:59.163
And for that, they rely on a powerful organelle
called the chlorosome.

00:05:59.163 --> 00:06:06.934
The chlorosome is unique to green sulfur bacteria,
and its entire purpose is to help the bacteria get light.

00:06:06.934 --> 00:06:11.880
It’s the shape of an oval and stuck to the
inside of the plasma membrane.

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:17.480
And inside the chlorosome are around 100,000
of the green sulfur bacteria’s

00:06:17.480 --> 00:06:19.669
photosynthetic pigments.

00:06:19.669 --> 00:06:24.504
Those pigment are compounds that 
aggregate together, creating the largest known

00:06:24.504 --> 00:06:27.665
light-harvesting complex in nature.

00:06:27.665 --> 00:06:32.889
And this arrangement is different from other familiar
complexes, where pigment compounds are arranged

00:06:32.889 --> 00:06:36.521
with proteins, and in much smaller numbers.

00:06:36.521 --> 00:06:42.099
In the green sulfur bacteria, the interactions
between these pigments make the bacteria very

00:06:42.099 --> 00:06:48.970
efficient at gathering light, and then transferring
that energy to a protein called the Fenna-Matthews

00:06:48.970 --> 00:06:55.060
Olson protein, which will help carry the energy
to the areas of the cell that need it for

00:06:55.060 --> 00:06:56.710
photosynthesis.

00:06:56.710 --> 00:07:00.479
So that even when they’re in the dark, the
green sulfur bacteria can get what they need

00:07:00.479 --> 00:07:01.866
to survive.

00:07:01.866 --> 00:07:06.990
Of course, sometimes your surroundings are
dark because you’re covered in purple sulfur bacteria.

00:07:06.990 --> 00:07:11.320
And sometimes they are dark because you are
in a hydrothermal vent.

00:07:11.320 --> 00:07:15.699
And for the scientists behind the 2005 paper
we mentioned at the beginning of the episode,

00:07:15.699 --> 00:07:21.794
the chlorosome would end up being a very useful
organelle to know about.

00:07:21.794 --> 00:07:28.284
They had gone to the East Pacific Rise, gathering
samples from plumes above the vents.

00:07:28.284 --> 00:07:34.970
And in their samples, they found a small,
green, nonmotile phototrophic sulfur bacteria.

00:07:34.970 --> 00:07:39.139
It looked a lot like a green sulfur bacteria.

00:07:39.139 --> 00:07:43.490
To confirm that it actually was a species
of green sulfur bacteria, they looked for

00:07:43.490 --> 00:07:49.629
that Fenna-Matthews Olson protein that transfers
energy from the chlorosome to the photosynthesis

00:07:49.629 --> 00:07:51.186
reaction centers.

00:07:51.186 --> 00:07:52.687
And there it was:

00:07:52.687 --> 00:08:01.007
a protein unique to green sulfur bacteria, a part 
of its ingenious methods for harvesting sunlight.

00:08:01.007 --> 00:08:04.117
But where was the sunlight?

00:08:07.939 --> 00:08:09.569
This is the weird part.

00:08:09.569 --> 00:08:13.675
The bacteria weren’t actually harvesting
sunlight.

00:08:13.675 --> 00:08:18.326
They were absorbing light from the hydrothermal
vent itself.

00:08:18.326 --> 00:08:26.516
The scalding temperatures of the vent produces
thermal radiation, also know as infrared light.

00:08:26.516 --> 00:08:32.479
And the green sulfur bacteria’s chlorosomes
were able to make enough use of that light

00:08:32.479 --> 00:08:35.243
to carry out photosynthesis.

00:08:35.243 --> 00:08:38.690
They weren’t, like thriving under these circumstances.

00:08:38.690 --> 00:08:44.242
But they were able to survive, and that is
no small feat.

00:08:44.242 --> 00:08:49.790
This actually wasn’t the first time scientists had
found photosynthetic bacteria in hydrothermal

00:08:49.790 --> 00:08:55.290
vents, but that previous find appeared to
only be getting extra energy to supplement

00:08:55.290 --> 00:08:59.106
its primary chemosynthetic source of food.

00:08:59.106 --> 00:09:06.141
This was the first time scientists had found
a species entirely dependent on photosynthesis

00:09:06.141 --> 00:09:07.959
at a hydrothermal vents.

00:09:07.959 --> 00:09:14.480
A species whose family is thought to survive
only in places with sun, and it had turned

00:09:14.480 --> 00:09:21.148
up deep in the ocean thanks to thousands of
pigment molecules that could take in

00:09:21.148 --> 00:09:24.790
geothermal radiation.

00:09:24.790 --> 00:09:28.950
In their paper, the scientists were quick
to caution that the discovery of green sulfur

00:09:28.950 --> 00:09:34.130
bacteria in the hydrothermal vents doesn’t
necessarily tell us much about the evolution

00:09:34.130 --> 00:09:36.700
of life in those environments.

00:09:36.700 --> 00:09:42.940
The ecosystems in these types of places are
always shifting around, and so we can’t know much

00:09:42.940 --> 00:09:47.890
from this one discovery about how the presence
of green sulfur bacteria fits within the

00:09:47.890 --> 00:09:50.620
history of the landscape 
and the life within it.

00:09:50.620 --> 00:09:56.540
But even if it cannot tell us the entirety
of the area’s past, the discovery of green

00:09:56.540 --> 00:10:02.510
sulfur bacteria in a hydrothermal vent shows
us just how broad nature can be in applying

00:10:02.510 --> 00:10:08.880
the structures it creates, transforming an
ancient process whose requirements we thought

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:13.686
we understood into something entirely different.

00:10:17.581 --> 00:10:23.680
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as 
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:10:23.680 --> 00:10:28.680
The names on the screen right now, they are
names of our Patreon patrons, people who help

00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:36.100
us continue exploring this really marvelous
planet that we all get to inhabit together.

00:10:36.100 --> 00:10:40.041
If you want to become one of them, you can
go to Patreon.com/JourneytoMicro.

00:10:40.041 --> 00:10:44.420
And we very much will appreciate it if you
do, it will allow us to keep making more and

00:10:44.420 --> 00:10:46.540
better videos like this one.

00:10:46.540 --> 00:10:49.880
If you want to see more from our Master of
